[section:release_notes Release Notes]

[heading Unreleased]

* Unified error mechanism. `taar::failure` is the one error value: an
  `error_code` from any category, a message for the occurrence,
  structured data, a nested cause, the source location and, when made
  from a foreign exception, that exception. `text()` renders it for
  people and `chain()` with category tags for logs; `as<E>()` returns the
  kept exception if it is an `E`.
* `taar::result<T>` (`boost::system::result<T, failure>`) is what
  argument providers and parsers return; `value()` on a failure throws
  `taar::exception`, the one exception type the library throws.
  `failure::from_exception` wraps anything else exactly once.
* Every error hook receives `taar::failure const&`: the session's soft
  and hard error handlers, the JSON-RPC error mapper and the MCP tool
  error handler. The defaults map by code: `http_status_for` for REST,
  `jsonrpc::default_error_mapper`, `mcp::default_tool_error_handler`.
  New taar code `error::unknown_exception`; new `mcp::error` category
  (`resource_not_found`, `request_cancelled`, `client_error`,
  `unknown_session`).
* Library errors carry messages that stand on their own: argument
  failures name the argument and its position and nest the provider or
  cast failure; body and multipart limits state the limit; MCP request
  timeouts state the method, session and timeout.
* MCP: ending a session (`DELETE`, end of stdio input, a replaced stdio
  session) closes its event stream and fails its pending server-to-client
  requests at once with `errc::operation_canceled`; before, they waited
  for `request_timeout`.
* Contexts: `session::basic_http<Context>` creates one context object per
  connection; `handler::rest` callables request it (and any other context
  in scope) through leading `T&`, `T const&` or `shared_ptr<T>` parameters.
* `core::context_store`: thread-safe keyed store with idle expiry,
  per-entry asynchronous locking and minted keys.
  `handler::with_session` binds a store to a cookie or header and injects
  the keyed context into the wrapped handler.
* JSON-RPC 2.0: `jsonrpc::endpoint<Context...>` with named parameters
  (`rpc_arg`), batches, notifications, standard error codes and a
  replaceable error mapper.
* MCP: `mcp::endpoint<Session, Context...>` over Streamable HTTP with
  tools, resources, URI templates, sessions, protocol version negotiation,
  Origin validation, and a server-to-client event stream (`stream_handler`,
  `notify`, `broadcast`).
* OpenAPI: `openapi::generator` renders an OpenAPI 3.1 document from routes
  registered with `describe(...)`; inexpressible matchers are rejected at
  compile time. `register_request_handler` accepts trailing registration
  observers.
* Argument providers accept descriptions; `rest` handlers expose `params()`.
  `response_from` accepts `boost::json::object` and `boost::json::array`.
* `core::async_channel`: executor-agnostic asynchronous queue used for
  server-push streams.
* JSON Schema generation for `boost::describe`d structs and enums,
  sequences and maps (`json_schema_for`), shared by OpenAPI and MCP;
  described types and JSON containers as REST return values.
* REST request providers (`header_arg`, `cookie_arg`, `query_arg`) accepted
  on JSON-RPC methods and MCP tools.
* `mcp::session_info` context; `mcp::endpoint::serve_stdio` transport.
* `core::cors` policy and `basic_http::set_response_filter`.
* Streaming request bodies: `body_reader`, `streaming_request` and the
  `stream_body_arg` provider; the body limit does not apply to them.
* `multipart/form-data`: `parse_multipart` and `multipart_form_arg`
  (buffered), `multipart_reader` and `multipart_stream_arg` (streamed).
* JSON-RPC positional parameters: a `params` array binds to `rpc_arg`
  providers in order.
* MCP resource templates accept RFC 6570 operators (`{+path}`, `{#f}`,
  `{.e}`, `{/s}`, `{;p}`, `{?q}`, `{&q}`, lists, `*`, `:n`); plain `{var}`
  no longer spans a slash.
* Review round 3: the keepalive timer cancels the stream's receive
  instead of racing it (no event can be dropped); `request()` waits are
  bounded by `server_info::request_timeout`; a pending `async_channel`
  receive keeps the `io_context` alive; `Content-Type` parameters such
  as `charset` no longer fail body providers; the accept loop backs off
  on persistent errors; the session lingers briefly on close so a
  response is not reset away; `cors` appends to `Vary`; `serve_stdio`
  handles a final line without a newline; `server::tcp`/`tls` refuse a
  temporary session at compile time; `request_scope::slot` documented as
  the endpoint's.
* Review round 2: arguments converted for a coroutine tool or method
  live for the whole call (a `T const&` parameter no longer dangles); a
  raw handler may take the TLS stream (no shutdown of the moved-from
  stream); the close sequence runs after a cancelled loop so TLS sends
  `close_notify` on shutdown; a stream interrupted by shutdown is a
  clean close, not a soft error; sessions stay movable after handlers
  are installed; registration is validated before observers record it;
  `path_arg` placeholders are accepted from any `||` target
  alternative; event ids and queue order agree under concurrent
  producers; idle event streams send keepalive comments
  (`stream_keepalive`); `{;a,b}` accepts pairs in any order;
  `async_channel` never touches a consumer's slot from a producer;
  key generation reuses a per-thread `random_device`.
* Review round 1: connections run on strands made from the `io_context`
  executor (not nested under the acceptor's); a slot bound to the server
  coroutine by its spawner stops it again; `serve_stdio` drains running
  calls on end of input; the undelivered MCP event queue is bounded by
  `stream_history`; `cors` never reflects a wildcard with credentials;
  protocol-error responses (400/413/431) go through the response filter;
  shutdown closes connections after `message_generator` responses too;
  `basic_http` can be moved after installing handlers;
  `context_store::create` draws and inserts keys under one lock;
  `async_channel` clears the consumer's cancellation slot on completion.
  Stateless MCP endpoints no longer advertise `logging`.
* MCP `logging/setLevel` with `log()`/`log_all()`, `completion/complete`
  with `complete_prompt`/`complete_resource`, and `tool_spec` for tool
  `title`, `annotations` and `outputSchema` (derived from the return type
  by default).
* Multi-threaded `io_context`: every connection runs on its own strand
  and `cancellation_signals::emit` dispatches through it, fixing a crash
  when shutting down a server driven by several threads. `taar::awaitable`
  now uses `any_io_executor`; completion tokens spelled with
  `io_context::executor_type` should use `taar::use_awaitable`.
  Binding `signals.slot()` to the server coroutine is no longer needed.
* `query_list_arg`: repeated query keys bind to a container, element by
  element; OpenAPI marks `query_arg` CSV lists as `explode: false`.
* `with_session` accepts streaming inner handlers (`chunked_response`,
  `async_generator`); `session_key::*::key_header`.
* `handler::constrained` argument wrapper: declarative validation with
  JSON Schema keywords on REST, JSON-RPC and MCP; `error::constraint_violated`.
* OpenAPI: `default` from `with_default`, security schemes
  (`add_security_scheme`, `set_security`, `operation::security`,
  inferred from credential headers), `components.schemas` holding the
  described types, referenced from operations (`set_inline_schemas` to
  opt out).
* MCP server-to-client requests: `endpoint::request`, `create_message`
  (sampling), `elicit`, `list_roots`; `client_capabilities` and
  `client_info` on `session_info`; `client_error`; `on_client_notification`.
* MCP binary content: `mcp::image`, `audio`, `embedded_resource`,
  `tool_result` and `blob` resources; `base64_encode`/`base64_decode`.
  SSE event ids with `Last-Event-ID` resumption.
* MCP prompts (`prompt()`, `prompts/list`, `prompts/get`), resource
  subscriptions (`resources/subscribe`, `resource_updated`), list-changed
  broadcasts, and `*/list` pagination via `server_info::page_size`.
* `jsonrpc::request_scope`: per-request id, cancellation and notification
  sink injectable into methods and tools; `endpoint::cancel(id)`; MCP
  `notifications/cancelled` and `notifications/progress`.
* `405 Method Not Allowed` with `Allow` for known paths, and `HEAD` served
  from `GET` routes with the body stripped; `set_method_not_allowed` and
  `set_head_from_get` switch them off. `matcher::route_shape` exposes the
  route analysis the OpenAPI generator and the session share.
* TLS: `basic_http` takes its stream type as a template parameter;
  `session::https` and `server::tls` serve HTTPS (OpenSSL required).
* `ROADMAP.md` lists the remaining gaps.
* `session::http`: responses follow the request's HTTP version and
  keep-alive; pipelined requests are served in order; a peer closing an
  idle connection is no longer reported as a hard error; shutdown lets
  in-flight handlers finish and closes afterwards; mid-stream generator
  exceptions reach the hard error handler.
* Test suite extended with user-scenario coverage (dual-surface
  consistency, transport and lifecycle e2e, streaming e2e, routing edge
  cases) and a compile-fail probe harness under `test/compile_fail/`.
* Matcher metadata: every matcher expression carries a compile-time
  description of its constraints and captured values.
* Examples reorganised around use cases (hello, REST CRUD with OpenAPI,
  connection state, cookie sessions, streaming, proxy, WebSocket, JSON-RPC,
  MCP).
* Initial release preparation. Headers relocated to
  `include/boost/taar/` to match the Boost modular layout.
* Added `build.jam`, `meta/libraries.json`, and a `doc/` directory built on
  QuickBook + Doxygen for integration with the Boost documentation build.

[heading Migration]

Breaking changes of the unified error mechanism:

* Hook signatures: `set_soft_error_handler`, `set_hard_error_handler`,
  `jsonrpc::endpoint::set_error_mapper` and
  `mcp::endpoint::set_tool_error_handler` take callables invoked with
  `taar::failure const&` instead of `std::exception_ptr`. Replace
  try/rethrow/catch ladders with `f.as<my_error>()`, `f.is(code)` or
  `f.has(code)`; `f.rethrow()` still throws the original exception.
* The library throws `taar::exception` where it threw
  `boost::system::system_error` or `std::system_error`: argument casts
  and the binder, `constrained`, cookie parsing, `parse_multipart`,
  `body_reader::read_all` and `multipart_reader::read_all` (with
  `body_limit`), streaming body transport errors, `chunked_response`
  late metadata, `path_arg` registration checks, and `server::tcp` /
  `server::tls` resolve, bind and listen failures. Catch `taar::exception` and inspect
  `e.error().code()`; the codes are unchanged.
* Argument providers return `taar::result<V>` instead of
  `boost::system::result<V>`; the not-found signal is
  `taar::failure{taar::error::argument_not_found}`.
  `matcher::parse_template` and `parse_cookies` also return
  `taar::result`. User providers returning `boost::system::result<V>`
  still work.
* The `mcp::client_error` exception class is removed. `request`,
  `elicit`, `create_message` and `list_roots` throw `taar::exception`
  whose failure carries `mcp::error::client_error` (data
  `{"code": n, "data": ...}`), `errc::timed_out`,
  `errc::operation_canceled` or `mcp::error::unknown_session`.
* `mcp::error` is now an error-code enum in `<boost/taar/mcp/error.hpp>`;
  resource-not-found and cancelled failures carry it and keep their wire
  codes (`-32002`, `-32800`).
* JSON-RPC `-32602` errors from argument failures carry `data` as the
  failure's object data plus `"message": text()` (for argument errors
  `{"argument": ..., "position": ..., "message": ...}`), or the `text()`
  string when there is no object data. `-32603` internal errors carry no
  `data`.
* REST default soft-error bodies are `failure::text()` (no category
  tags), e.g. `argument page (position 1) is invalid (caused by: ...)`;
  failures that do not map to a `4xx` status escalate to the hard error
  handler.

[heading 0.0.46]

* `cmake.test()` is used in the Conan recipe instead of a hand-built test
  executable path.

[heading 0.0.44]

* `session::http`: surface mid-stream chunked errors;
  harden `flatten_awaiter`.
* MSVC `/bigobj` for heavy templates and a raw-handler test fix on Windows.

[heading 0.0.43]

* `session::http`: add `register_raw_handler` for protocol upgrades such
  as WebSocket.

[endsect]
